::meta(crate: catasto-core, dependencies: (catasto-digest, catasto-git, catasto-manifest), dependents: (catasto-cli))

=[catasto-core]

`catasto-core` -- core engine and models for filesystem schemas, manifests, and lockfiles.

Just as a cadastre (@em[catasto]) records the boundaries and parcels of land,
`catasto` defines and verifies directory rules, naming conventions, media
manifests, and content lockfiles across a workspace.

A workspace declares the files and repositories it manages in `catasto.ctmt`.
\[`lock`\] records their SHA-256 and commits in `catasto.lock`, \[`check`\] reports
drift, and \[`sync`\] re-fetches them from their sources.

==[Module discovery]

Workspace discovery across directory trees.

===[Functions]

====[fn is_ignored_dir]

Returns true if a directory name matches a VCS or build artifact directory.

====[fn normalize_path]

Normalizes a path by stripping leading `./` curdir components for cleaner display.

====[fn discover_workspaces]

Discovers every catasto workspace under `root`, `root` itself included.

A workspace is a directory that holds a `catasto.ctmt`. `root` counts when it holds one, and the search goes on
below it and below every workspace it finds: a manifest never hides another one. `--all` means "every
`catasto.ctmt` under the root", and a workspace that sits inside another is still its own (its manifest and lock
are read from its own directory).

The search skips VCS directories and build artifacts (`.git`, `.jj`, `.agents`, `target`, `node_modules`,
`result`, `dist`, `.cache`, `tmp`) and does not follow symlinks.

The result is sorted, so `root` comes first and the rest follow in a fixed order.

==[Module submodule]

`.gitmodules` generation and verification for repository entries.

===[Enums]

====[enum GitmodulesDrift]

What kind of drift `.gitmodules` has compared to `catasto.ctmt`.

===[Functions]

====[fn render_gitmodules]

Renders `.gitmodules` text from the manifest, or `None` if no entry has `submodule: true`.

====[fn sync_gitmodules]

Synchronizes `.gitmodules` on disk with `manifest`.

Writes the file if `submodule: true` entries exist; removes it if previously tracked submodules are gone.
Leaves an unrelated `.gitmodules` untouched when neither `manifest` nor `lock` declares any submodule.

====[fn check_gitmodules]

Checks whether `.gitmodules` has drifted from `manifest`.

===[Constants]

====[const GITMODULES_FILE]
